from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(9,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("applied-chemistry","Asha's grandmother took a tablet and said, \"This is chemistry in your hands.\" Asha wanted to know how many ways chemistry helps us.",
 ["Nanochemistry deals with materials of extremely small size, nanoparticles, whose properties differ from those of the same material in bulk. They have many applications, though they also have drawbacks. Pharmaceutical chemistry is about drugs. A drug is a substance used to modify or explore body systems for the benefit of the person. Anaesthetics cause loss of sensation and antibiotics fight bacteria.",
  "Electrochemistry uses an electrochemical cell, a device in which a chemical change produces electricity or electricity brings about a chemical change. It has two electrodes, the anode and the cathode. Radiochemistry studies radioactive decay, the change of an unstable radioisotope into another nucleus, with uses in medicine and industry.",
  "Dye chemistry deals with substances that colour materials. Agricultural chemistry looks at fertilisers and pesticides and food chemistry at nutrients and preservatives. Forensic chemists examine evidence from crime scenes using scientific methods."],
 ["nanoparticles","drug","electrochemical cell","radioisotope","dye","forensic chemistry"],
 ("Name the two electrodes of an electrochemical cell.",["An electrochemical cell has two electrodes.","One is called the anode.","The other is the cathode."]),
 ("Antibiotics are used to cure viral colds.",["Antibiotics act on bacteria.","They do not cure viral infections."]),
 ["Nanochemistry studies very tiny particles.","A drug modifies body functions.","Cells have an anode and a cathode.","Forensic chemistry helps solve crimes."],
 ["m|A drug that causes loss of sensation is a|anaesthetic|antibiotic|dye|Used in surgery.","t|A radioisotope undergoes radioactive decay.|T|Yes.","m|A device that turns chemical change to electricity is an|electrochemical cell|nanoparticle|crystal|A battery.","t|Forensic chemistry is the study of dyes.|F|It deals with crime evidence.","m|Nanoparticles are|extremely small|very large|gases only|Smaller than 100 nm.","s|Which electrode of a cell is the other of the anode?|cathode|Cathode.","m|Chemistry of colouring substances is|dye chemistry|food chemistry|forensic chemistry|Dyes colour cloth.","t|Antibiotics are drugs.|T|Yes."])
C("animal-kingdom","Raju saw an earthworm, a fish and a sparrow on one day. \"How do scientists sort so many animals?\" he asked.",
 ["Classification is the arrangement of organisms into groups on the basis of similarities, differences and relationships. Taxonomy is the science of classification. The levels run from kingdom, the highest, down to species, the lowest category. In binomial nomenclature each organism gets two names: the genus, written with a capital letter, then the species in small letters.",
  "Animals are grouped by features such as germ layers. Animals with two germ layers, the ectoderm and the endoderm, are diploblastic, and those with three layers (ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm) are triploblastic. The main split is between invertebrates, which have no notochord, and chordates, which have a notochord or backbone.",
  "Invertebrate phyla include sponges, coelenterates, flatworms, roundworms, segmented worms like earthworms and leeches, arthropods with jointed legs, molluscs and the spiny-skinned animals such as starfish. Arthropoda is the largest phylum, and its members cast off and regrow their exoskeleton, which is moulting. Chordates include fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals."],
 ["classification","taxonomy","binomial nomenclature","invertebrates","arthropoda","chordates"],
 ("Write the correct form of the scientific name of the human.",["Genus is written first with a capital letter.","Species follows in small letters.","Homo sapiens."]),
 ("Chordates are animals without a backbone.",["Chordates have a notochord or backbone.","Animals without it are invertebrates."]),
 ["Taxonomy is the science of classification.","The species is the lowest category.","Invertebrates lack a notochord.","Arthropoda is the largest phylum."],
 ["m|The highest category of classification is|kingdom|species|genus|Kingdom is the largest.","t|Binomial nomenclature gives each species two names.|T|Genus and species.","m|The largest phylum is|Arthropoda|Porifera|Annelida|Jointed-legged animals.","t|Earthworms are chordates.|F|They are invertebrates.","m|Animals with a backbone are|chordates|arthropods|annelids|They have a notochord.","s|What is casting off the exoskeleton called?|moulting|Moulting.","m|Triploblastic animals have|three germ layers|two germ layers|one germ layer|Ecto, meso and endoderm.","t|Species is the lowest taxonomic category.|T|Yes."])
C("organisation-of-tissues","Meena looked at an onion peel under the microscope and saw neat little boxes. \"Those are cells,\" said her teacher, \"and groups of similar cells make tissues.\"",
 ["A tissue is a group of similar cells doing a particular job. Plant tissues are of two kinds. Meristematic tissues are made of cells that keep dividing and cause growth, found at the tips of roots and shoots. Permanent tissues have cells that have stopped dividing and are simple or complex, such as xylem that carries water and phloem that carries food.",
  "Animal tissues are of four main types. Epithelial tissue covers body surfaces and lines organs. Connective tissue joins and supports parts, as in blood, bone and cartilage. Muscular tissue moves the body by contracting. Nervous tissue carries messages as impulses.",
  "Cells multiply by cell division. In mitosis one cell divides to make two identical cells for growth and repair, and in meiosis the chromosome number is halved to make gametes."],
 ["meristematic tissue","permanent tissue","epithelial tissue","connective tissue","muscular tissue","mitosis"],
 ("Which plant tissue carries water from roots to leaves?",["Complex permanent tissues carry materials.","Xylem carries water.","So the answer is xylem."]),
 ("Muscular tissue carries messages to the brain.",["Nervous tissue carries messages.","Muscular tissue contracts to move parts."]),
 ["Tissues are groups of similar cells.","Meristematic tissue grows by dividing.","Animals have four main tissues.","Mitosis makes identical cells."],
 ["m|Growing tips of plants have|meristematic tissue|muscle|bone|Cells keep dividing there.","t|Xylem carries water.|T|Yes.","m|Tissue that covers body surfaces is|epithelial|nervous|muscular|It forms skin and linings.","t|Blood is a connective tissue.|T|It is a fluid connective tissue.","m|Cell division that gives two identical cells is|mitosis|meiosis|digestion|It helps growth.","s|Which tissue carries food in plants?|phloem|Phloem.","m|Tissue that sends nerve impulses is|nervous tissue|bone|cartilage|It carries messages.","t|Meiosis produces gametes.|T|It halves the chromosomes."])
C("plant-physiology","Asha saw a sunflower turning its face to the Sun and a touch-me-not plant closing its leaves. \"Plants respond too!\" she said.",
 ["A growth movement whose direction depends on the direction of the stimulus is called tropism, as in phototropism towards light, geotropism towards gravity and hydrotropism towards water. A non-directional response to a stimulus, like the closing of touch-me-not leaves, is a nastic movement.",
  "Photosynthesis is the process by which plants prepare their food. It needs sunlight, chlorophyll, carbon dioxide and water, and it gives out oxygen. The glucose made is the food of the plant.",
  "Transpiration is the loss of water as water vapour from the aerial parts of the plant, mainly through stomata on the leaf. Stomata also exchange gases. Transpiration pulls water up the plant and cools the leaf."],
 ["tropism","nastic movement","photosynthesis","chlorophyll","transpiration","stomata"],
 ("What four things does photosynthesis need?",["Plants need light energy.","They need chlorophyll, carbon dioxide and water.","So: sunlight, chlorophyll, carbon dioxide and water."]),
 ("Nastic movements depend on the direction of the stimulus.",["Nastic movements are non-directional.","Tropism depends on direction."]),
 ["Tropism is directional growth.","Photosynthesis makes food using light.","Transpiration is loss of water vapour.","Stomata allow gas exchange."],
 ["m|Bending of a shoot towards light is|phototropism|geotropism|hydrotropism|Photo means light.","t|Photosynthesis gives out oxygen.|T|Yes.","m|Loss of water vapour from leaves is|transpiration|respiration|digestion|Through stomata.","t|Touch-me-not closing is a tropism.|F|It is a nastic movement.","m|Tiny pores on leaves are|stomata|roots|veins|They open and close.","s|Which green pigment traps sunlight?|chlorophyll|Chlorophyll.","m|Photosynthesis needs|carbon dioxide and water|only oxygen|only soil|With light.","t|Roots bend towards water in hydrotropism.|T|Yes."])
